    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what? I'm not sure I follow the goblin comparison. Can you just stick to the technical side? Specifically, what are the actual physical mechanisms behind the pressure differential, and what kind of specific blockages usually cause the airspeed indicator to fail?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's dive into the technical side of things.\n\nThe pitot tube works on the principle of pressure differential. As the aircraft moves forward, the air flowing over the wing creates a pressure difference between the front and rear of the tube. The pitot tube is designed to capture this pressure difference, and the resulting pressure is transmitted to the airspeed indicator.\n\nThe airspeed indicator, typically a pitot-static system, uses a combination of static ports and a pitot tube to measure the airspeed. The static ports are usually located on the sides of the aircraft, and they measure the air pressure at a fixed point. The pitot tube, on the other hand, measures the air pressure at the front of the aircraft.\n\nWhen the air flows over the wing, it creates a higher pressure at the front of the pitot tube compared to the static ports. This pressure difference is transmitted to the airspeed indicator, which then calculates the airspeed based on the pressure readings.\n\nNow, when it comes to blockages, the most common issues are:\n\n1. Ice accumulation: Ice can form on the pitot tube, reducing the pressure difference and causing the airspeed indicator to become inaccurate.",
